  "raw": " replaced by John Cornbury. Like Newton who was succeeded as Tory leader this year, Burke set up a rival party called 'Progressive'. The angry reaction to the trial led to violent disorders - even several National Guards were killed; shooting happened at Poole Bridge, Balham Road and you name it! Burke himself had little difficulty in escaping unscathed but one fellow commuter certainly suffered - Eustace (Fanny) Edgeworth didn't make it after thirty days in hospital in an underground bunker upstairs on Aldersgate Street - she died on 18 July, just three weeks short of her 31st birthday.\nWhat can account for these widely varying reactions to January Joans? How could London life have been different if half-a-dozen gun emplacements dotted around its villages hadn’t caused such widespread havoc two months later? This is not quite what Burke meant when he wrote about his problem with urban violence: “the common quarrel between a man and women became again suspended",
  "serialized": "replaced by John Cornbury. Like Newton who was succeeded as Tory leader this year, Burke set up a rival party called 'Progressive'. The angry reaction to the trial led to violent disorders - even several National Guards were killed; shooting happened at Poole Bridge, Balham Road and you name it! Burke himself had little difficulty in escaping unscathed but one fellow commuter certainly suffered - Eustace (Fanny) Edgeworth didn't make it after thirty days in hospital in an underground bunker upstairs on Aldersgate Street - she died on 18 July, just three weeks short of her 31st birthday. What can account for these widely varying reactions to January Joans? How could London life have been different if half-a-dozen gun emplacements dotted around its villages hadn’t caused such widespread havoc two months later? This is not quite what Burke meant when he wrote about his problem with urban violence: “the common quarrel between a man and women became again suspended",
